from pathlib import Path
import json,re
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
E=[]
def source(file,needle):
 records=json.loads((P/file).read_text())
 for i,r in enumerate(records):
  for c in r.get('result',{}).get('content',[]):
   if c.get('type')=='text':
    for line in c['text'].splitlines():
     if needle in line:return line,f'{file} record {i}'
 raise ValueError(needle)
def add(n,title,date,evidence,receipt,needle,question,summary,definitions,name,statement,proof,setup,calculation,conclusion,limits,extension,refs):
 excerpt,loc=source(receipt,needle)
 E.append(dict(id=f'Y25-05-{n:03d}',title=title,status='Developed mathematical repair or classical result; speculative source claims delimited',summary=summary,definitions=definitions,result=dict(name=name,statement=statement,proof=proof),example=dict(setup=setup,calculation=calculation,conclusion=conclusion),limits=limits,extension=extension,references=[dict(label=a,url=b) for a,b in refs],original=dict(date=date,month='2025-05',evidence=evidence,field=title,concept=title,origin='Recovered dated mathematical source fragment; external file context only when evidence T',question='Reconstruction: '+question,math=statement,finish='Precise hypotheses, proof or derivation, worked example and finite verification',kind='Revised mathematical treatment',refs=[b for a,b in refs],source_excerpt=excerpt,retrieval_receipt=loc,original_question=None,attribution_note='The source excerpt is the exact retrieved summary line, not a claimed verbatim historical prompt. The revised question, proof and example are newly authored. U identifies retrieved user attribution, A prior assistant output, and T external context rather than a recovered user conversation.')))
add(1,'The Mexican-hat Fourier pair needs a declared normalization','2025-05-04','T','root_receipts.json','**May 4, 2025',
 'Which Gaussian derivative gives a consistent Mexican-hat Fourier pair, what moments vanish, and what admissibility integral follows?',
 'The external May4 file contains a Mexican-hat formula and Fourier transform that do not match under the usual unnormalized forward transform. This repair fixes the convention before proving cancellation and admissibility. The file context is retained as T; no historical user prompt or completed wavelet theorem is invented.',
 ['Use F[f](ω)=∫_R f(x)e^(−iωx)dx. Let Gσ(x)=(√(2π)σ)^(−1)e^(−x²/(2σ²)), σ>0, so F[Gσ]=e^(−σ²ω²/2).',
 'Define ψσ=−Gσ″=(√(2π)σ³)^(−1)(1−x²/σ²)e^(−x²/(2σ²)). These functions and their derivatives decay rapidly.'],
 'A consistent pair, two vanishing moments and finite admissibility',
 'F[ψσ](ω)=ω²e^(−σ²ω²/2), ∫ψσ=∫xψσ=0, and ∫_R|F[ψσ](ω)|²/|ω|dω=σ^(−4). The source coefficient 1/(2πσ³) would instead give an extra factor 1/√(2π) with this transform convention.',
 'Integration by parts gives F[f″]=−ω²F[f] when boundary terms vanish; apply it to Gσ. Integrating −Gσ″ directly gives zero. Integrating −xGσ″ by parts also gives zero because the remaining integral is a boundary difference of Gσ. Thus the Fourier transform vanishes to second order at frequency zero. For admissibility, symmetry reduces the integral to 2∫₀∞ω³e^(−σ²ω²)dω. With u=σ²ω² this is σ^(−4)∫₀∞ue^(−u)du=σ^(−4). All integrations converge absolutely. Finally the ratio of the source’s spatial coefficient to the corrected one is 1/√(2π); linearity of the transform forces the same ratio on the frequency side. A different Fourier convention could change constants, but it must be stated consistently on both sides.',
 'Take σ=1 and compare the corrected wavelet with the retrieved spatial coefficient.',
 'The corrected value at zero is 1/√(2π), and the retrieved value is 1/(2π). The corrected frequency response at ω=1 is e^(−1/2), whereas the retrieved spatial normalization transforms to e^(−1/2)/√(2π). The admissibility integral is exactly one. The verifier evaluates Gaussian moments independently to check the two cancellations and checks the analytic normalization ratio.',
 'A normalization error is repaired without promoting the file’s completeness or novel-invariant claims into proved results.',
 'This proves a continuous Fourier calculation, not completeness or stable inversion of an arbitrary discrete translation/dilation grid. The file’s Bui–Laugesen attribution and numerical reconstruction constant need their exact theorem and sampling hypotheses.',
 'For a specified discrete grid, estimate lower and upper frame bounds rather than assuming that continuous admissibility implies stable discrete reconstruction. The cited completeness results are separate theorems with their own spaces and scale conditions.',
 [('Bui and Laugesen, wavelets and Mexican-hat completeness','https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/wavelets-in-littlewood-paley-space-and-mexican-hat-completeness'),('Bui and Laugesen, frequency-scale frames','https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/frequency-scale-frames-and-the-solution-of-the-mexican-hat-proble')])
add(2,'Curl flux is a boundary quantity before it is a topological invariant','2025-05-04','T','root_receipts.json','**May 4, 2025',
 'What does a curl-flux integral actually determine, and which extra hypotheses would make it independent of the chosen surface?',
 'The external topic label combines a wavelet with curl-flux integrals and proposed topological invariants. A precise first step is Stokes’ theorem. It explains what is surface independent, what changes with the vector field, and why an arbitrary real-valued integral is not automatically a topological invariant.',
 ['Let S be a compact oriented piecewise smooth surface in R³ with oriented boundary C. Let F be C¹ on a neighborhood of S.',
 'Define Φ_F(S)=∫_S(curl F)·n dA. Boundary orientation is chosen by the usual right-hand convention. For a closed surface assume the additional smoothness needed for the divergence theorem.'],
 'Stokes independence with fixed boundary, and a counterexample to topological invariance',
 'Φ_F(S)=∮_C F·dr. Consequently two admissible surfaces with the same oriented boundary give the same integral for the same field. The value can nevertheless vary continuously under changes of F while the surface topology stays fixed.',
 'Stokes’ theorem is the explicit classical input identifying the surface integral with circulation. Applying it to S₁ and S₂ with the same C gives equal values, provided F is defined smoothly on both. For a closed surface bounding a region with a smooth field, divergence of curl is zero, so its flux is zero; this also follows from Stokes because the boundary is empty. To show that no nontrivial topological invariant follows merely from the integral, use F_a(x,y,z)=a(−y,x,0)/2. Its curl is (0,0,a). On the unit disk in the xy-plane the flux is aπ, which ranges over all real values as a changes. The disk’s topology has not changed. The equality with circulation still holds, so the failure is in the proposed interpretation rather than in Stokes’ theorem.',
 'Use the upward-oriented disk of radius two and F=(−y,x,0)/2.',
 'The curl is (0,0,1), so the flux is the area 4π. Parameterize its boundary by r(t)=(2cos t,2sin t,0). Then F(r(t))=(−sin t,cos t,0) and F·r′=2, giving ∫₀^(2π)2dt=4π independently. Reversing orientation changes the sign. Scaling F by three gives 12π without changing the disk or boundary shape.',
 'The calculation gives a complete boundary certificate and identifies exactly why the adjective topological needs further structure.',
 'Singular fields, punctured domains and nonbounding cycles can carry additional information; the smooth-neighborhood assumptions cannot be dropped silently. No new invariant of knots or wavelets is proved here.',
 'Replace the arbitrary vector field by a closed differential form with a specified integral cohomology class. Then periods on homologous cycles agree, and integer normalization can give genuinely topological data. The closedness and integral-class assumptions are the required mathematical work.',
 [('MIT multivariable calculus, Stokes theorem','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-02sc-multivariable-calculus-fall-2010/')])
add(3,'A primitive fourth-power counterexample defeats the proposed exclusion','2025-05-09T19:22:59Z','A','root_receipts.json','A@2025-05-09T19:22:59Z',
 'Which claimed Euler-type exclusions already fail by exact arithmetic, and what does the correct number φ(1680)=384 actually establish?',
 'The recovered assistant framework combined a correct totient computation with an exclusion of primitive fourth-power solutions. Exact integer arithmetic separates them immediately. A categorical dimension or a numerical spectral pattern cannot override an explicit counterexample to the underlying Diophantine statement.',
 ['A primitive positive solution of a⁴+b⁴+c⁴=d⁴ has gcd(a,b,c,d)=1. Euler’s classical exclusion of three fourth powers is false.',
 'Euler’s totient is φ(n)=n∏_{p|n}(1−1/p). The cyclotomic field Q(ζ_n) has degree φ(n), a classical algebraic theorem.'],
 'An exact primitive counterexample and a local parity restriction',
 '95800⁴+217519⁴+414560⁴=422481⁴ and the four integers have gcd one. Also φ(1680)=384. The latter number does not imply the absence of the former solution. Any primitive solution has exactly one odd number among a,b,c and has d odd.',
 'The fourth-power equality and gcd are finite integer certificates, checked without rounding by the verifier. For the parity statement, every fourth power is zero or one modulo sixteen. Thus the number of odd summands on the left, an integer from zero through three, must equal zero or one modulo sixteen according to the parity of d. It is therefore zero or one exactly. If all three left entries were even, d would also be even, violating primitivity. Exactly one must be odd and d must be odd. For the totient, factor 1680=2⁴·3·5·7 and multiply 1680(1/2)(2/3)(4/5)(6/7)=384. The angle π/840 is indeed 2π/1680, compatible with a primitive 1680th root. These correct cyclotomic facts do not create a logical implication about the quartic equation; an additional theorem would have to state and prove that bridge, and the explicit counterexample prevents the proposed exclusion.',
 'Check the displayed solution and its congruence pattern, then distinguish a fifth-power example.',
 'The quartic solution has even, odd, even left entries and an odd right entry, exactly as required. Independently 27⁵+84⁵+110⁵+133⁵=144⁵ disproves the corresponding four-term exclusion for fifth powers. That example has four summands, so it neither proves nor disproves a separate assertion about exactly three fifth powers.',
 'Term count, exponent and primitivity are tracked explicitly, preventing a correct nearby fact from supporting an incorrect conjecture.',
 'The finite certificates do not classify all solutions or settle every higher-exponent equation. The historical proposed absence of three-term solutions for all k≥5 is not established by its spectral or motivic labels.',
 'Construct congruence filters and certify each surviving exact candidate, then study a specified parametrized quartic family. Any claimed complete exclusion must survive these known solutions before additional machinery is introduced.',
 [('Elkies, On A⁴+B⁴+C⁴=D⁴','https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1988-0930224-9'),('Elkies mathematical publications','https://people.math.harvard.edu/~elkies/math_pubs.html')])
add(4,'The periodic prime-grid Laplacian has an exact Fourier spectrum','2025-05-14T16:27:05Z','A','root_followups.json','A@2025-05-14T16:27:05Z',
 'What are the sign, kernel and spectral gap of the stated periodic p³-grid operator, and which asymptotics genuinely follow from its formula?',
 'The exact periodic operator is one of the grounded parts of the May14 framework. Its sign must be fixed before writing a heat semigroup or a spectral gap. Primality of p is unnecessary for the diagonalization: the same calculation works for every integer p≥3.',
 ['On G=(Z/pZ)³ let (Lu)(x)=Σ_{j=1}³[u(x+e_j)+u(x−e_j)−2u(x)]. Define the positive operator A=−L.',
 'For k∈G use normalized Fourier characters χ_k(x)=p^(−3/2)exp(2πik·x/p). Orthogonality is with the finite counting inner product.'],
 'Complete eigenvalues and the gap asymptotic',
 'Lχ_k=−4Σ_j sin²(πk_j/p)χ_k, so A is positive semidefinite with a one-dimensional constant kernel. Its smallest positive eigenvalue is γ_p=4sin²(π/p)∼4π²/p². The contractive heat evolution is e^(−tA)=e^(tL), not e^(−tL).',
 'A shift by ±e_j multiplies χ_k by e^(±2πik_j/p). Adding the two shifts and subtracting two gives 2cos(2πk_j/p)−2=−4sin²(πk_j/p). Summing proves the eigenvalue formula. The character inner products are products of geometric sums, zero unless the indices agree, so these p³ characters form a full orthonormal basis. Every eigenvalue of A is nonnegative, and it vanishes only when all k_j=0 modulo p. The least positive sine square occurs at k_j=±1 in one coordinate with the other coordinates zero. The limit sin z/z→1 proves the asymptotic. In this eigenbasis the norm of e^(−tA) is one, while its restriction to mean-zero functions has norm e^(−tγ_p). The opposite sign e^(−tL) amplifies nonconstant modes and is not a dissipative heat operator.',
 'Take p=4 as a transparent exact check, even though this grid size is not prime.',
 'Each one-coordinate contribution to A is 0,2,4,2. Thus the three-dimensional eigenvalues are sums of three such entries, the kernel is constants and the gap is two. The eigenvalue at k=(1,1,1) is six. The verifier constructs the 64-site matrix and Fourier-mode residuals independently, and checks the exact row sum and quadratic-form identity.',
 'The actual operator yields a p^(−2) gap. A different logarithmic gap law needs a different, fully specified graph and cannot be inferred from arithmetic terminology.',
 'This proves no GUE spacing law, Ext dimension, motivic correspondence or learned prime-gap performance. The source’s later correction explicitly required separating those claims from this elementary Fourier theorem.',
 'For variable positive edge weights, retain the incidence-factorization A=BᵀWB and compare its Rayleigh quotient with the constant-weight operator. Uniform weight bounds give rigorous gap enclosures without guessing a new density exponent.',
 [('MIT Fourier and circulant linear algebra','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-06sc-linear-algebra-fall-2011/'),('NIST DLMF trigonometric identities','https://dlmf.nist.gov/4.21')])
add(5,'Galerkin projection explains what a coarse correction can guarantee','2025-05-14T16:28:34Z','A','root_followups.json','A@2025-05-14T16:28:34Z',
 'Which transfer-map and Krylov hypotheses make a coarse correction an energy projection rather than a claimed automatic convergence accelerator?',
 'A named interpolation or Krylov space does not by itself give a convergence theorem. For a positive definite operator the Galerkin construction does give a precise best-approximation property. This is the finite mathematical core shared by coarse-grid correction and projection onto a Krylov subspace.',
 ['Let A∈R^{n×n} be symmetric positive definite and P∈R^{n×r} have full column rank. Define A_c=PᵀAP and Π=P A_c^(−1)PᵀA.',
 'Use the energy inner product ⟨x,y⟩_A=xᵀAy. A Krylov space K_m(A,v)=span(v,Av,…,A^(m−1)v) is a particular candidate subspace; choose independent columns before forming P.'],
 'An exact energy projection and its limitation',
 'A_c is positive definite, Π²=Π and Π is A-self-adjoint. The coarse correction e↦(I−Π)e satisfies ||(I−Π)e||_A=min_z||e−Pz||_A≤||e||_A. This alone need not contract errors in the A-orthogonal complement of range(P).',
 'For z≠0, zᵀA_cz=(Pz)ᵀA(Pz)>0 because P is injective. Multiplying the formula for Π by itself cancels PᵀAP=A_c and proves idempotence. Also AΠ=AP A_c^(−1)PᵀA is symmetric, which is precisely energy self-adjointness. The normal equation PᵀA(e−Pz)=0 has solution z=A_c^(−1)PᵀAe. Splitting e−Pz into its projected and orthogonal pieces proves the Pythagorean identity and the minimization claim. If PᵀAe=0 then Πe=0, so the same error is unchanged. A multigrid rate therefore requires an additional smoothing/approximation argument; transfer dimensions and a subspace name cannot supply it.',
 'Choose A=diag(1,4), P=(1,1)ᵀ and e=(1,0)ᵀ.',
 'Then A_c=5, Π=[[1/5,4/5],[1/5,4/5]], and (I−Π)e=(4/5,−1/5). Its squared energy is 16/25+4/25=4/5, less than the original one. The vector (−4,1) is A-orthogonal to P and remains unchanged. The verifier checks idempotence, A-self-adjointness and both examples exactly with fractions.',
 'The useful guarantee is an energy best approximation with a clearly identified untouched complement.',
 'A periodic Laplacian has a constant kernel, so apply this positive-definite argument on its mean-zero subspace or fix a gauge first. Arbitrary transfer maps, singular coarse matrices or nonsymmetric operators require modified hypotheses.',
 'Combine this projection with a specified smoother S and prove ||(I−Π)S||_A≤q<1. A bound uniform over grid size would then be a genuine multilevel convergence theorem, stronger than a finite residual decrease.',
 [('MIT positive definite matrices and minima','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-06sc-linear-algebra-fall-2011/pages/positive-definite-matrices-and-applications/positive-definite-matrices-and-minima/')])
add(6,'Functional-calculus and Newton error bounds need explicit domains','2025-05-14T16:29:47Z','A','root_receipts.json','A@2025-05-14T16:29:47Z',
 'How does scalar approximation transfer to a symmetric matrix, and what local assumptions actually prove quadratic Newton convergence?',
 'Rational approximation and nonlinear iteration were named in the adaptive framework, but the retrieved exponential approximation bound is truncated. Two modest exact statements replace that missing certificate: a spectral maximum bound and a local Newton remainder estimate. Neither relies on an unrecorded Zolotarev constant.',
 ['Let A be real symmetric with spectrum in [a,b]. Let r be a rational function with no pole on that interval and f continuous there.',
 'For Newton’s method let F be C² near a root α, assume |F′|≥m>0 and |F″|≤M on an interval containing α and the current iterate, and assume the next iterates remain in that interval.'],
 'Spectral error transfer and a local quadratic recurrence',
 '||f(A)−r(A)||₂=max_{λ∈spectrum(A)}|f(λ)−r(λ)|≤sup_[a,b]|f−r|. Newton’s update x⁺=x−F(x)/F′(x) satisfies |x⁺−α|≤[M/(2m)]|x−α|² under the stated local bounds.',
 'An orthogonal diagonalization A=Udiag(λ_i)Uᵀ makes f(A)−r(A) orthogonally similar to the diagonal matrix of scalar errors; its Euclidean operator norm is the largest absolute diagonal entry. The pole exclusion makes r(A) well defined. For Newton, Taylor-expand F(α)=F(x)+F′(x)(α−x)+F″(ξ)(α−x)²/2 for some intermediate ξ. Rearranging and using F(α)=0 gives x⁺−α=F″(ξ)(x−α)²/[2F′(x)], up to an irrelevant sign, proving the absolute bound. Remaining inside the interval is a real hypothesis: a local error estimate is not global convergence from every initial point.',
 'Use A=diag(1,2), f(λ)=1/λ and r(λ)=3/2−λ/2. Separately use F(x)=x²−2 and x₀=3/2.',
 'The polynomial r is exact at both eigenvalues, so r(A)=A^(−1), although it is not exact throughout [1,2]. Newton gives x₁=17/12 and x₂=577/408. The exact residuals are x₁²−2=1/144 and x₂²−2=1/166464. These are also Pell-type rational certificates, computed without floating-point cancellation.',
 'A finite spectrum can make a simple approximation exact, while iteration convergence requires a separate nonlinear argument.',
 'No unrecovered uniform rational-approximation rate, prime-grid optimization complexity or arbitrary-start Newton guarantee is asserted. A residual-to-error bound additionally needs a derivative or inverse-conditioning estimate.',
 'For a prescribed positive interval [a,b], optimize a pole-free rational approximation and certify its scalar uniform error. That certificate then transfers immediately to every symmetric matrix with spectrum in the interval, independently of dimension.',
 [('NIST DLMF rational approximation','https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.11'),('MIT numerical analysis Newton method','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-335j-introduction-to-numerical-methods-spring-2019/')])
add(7,'Tripartite information vanishes for every pure three-party state','2025-05-18T02:28:11Z','A','root_receipts.json','A@2025-05-18T02:28:11Z',
 'Can tripartite information distinguish pure GHZ and W states, and what can be concluded without the missing family ψ(g)?',
 'The source’s GHZ endpoint is consistent, but a slightly negative tripartite-information caveat for a pure W endpoint is not. For a pure state of exactly three parties, the quantity vanishes identically. This is a structural obstruction to using it as the proposed transition marker, independent of numerical precision.',
 ['For a density matrix ρ define S(ρ)=−tr(ρlog₂ρ), with 0log0=0. For a pure state on A⊗B⊗C define I₃=S_A+S_B+S_C−S_AB−S_AC−S_BC+S_ABC.',
 'GHZ=(|000⟩+|111⟩)/√2 and W=(|001⟩+|010⟩+|100⟩)/√3. The retrieved parameterized state ψ(g) was not supplied.'],
 'Pure-state cancellation and unequal entanglement spectra',
 'Every finite-dimensional pure three-party state has I₃=0. GHZ has all nontrivial one-party and two-party entropies equal to one; W has them equal to h₂(1/3)≈0.9182958341. The two states therefore share I₃ despite different reduced spectra.',
 'The Schmidt decomposition across A versus BC gives identical nonzero eigenvalues for ρ_A and ρ_BC, so S_A=S_BC. The other two bipartitions give S_B=S_AC and S_C=S_AB. Purity gives S_ABC=0. Substituting cancels every term. For GHZ, tracing two qubits leaves diag(1/2,1/2). For W it leaves diag(2/3,1/3); permutation symmetry makes this true for each party. The two-party entropies follow from the same Schmidt argument, avoiding a larger eigenproblem. This proves exact zero rather than an approximately small or slightly negative value.',
 'As a declared new comparison family take ψ(θ)=cosθ|000⟩+sinθ|111⟩ for 0≤θ≤π/2.',
 'Every one-party entropy is h₂(sin²θ), every two-party entropy is the same, and I₃=0 throughout. At θ=0 it is a product; at θ=π/4 it is GHZ. The entropy changes continuously, while I₃ contains no information about this change. The verifier checks the reduced diagonal eigenvalues for GHZ/W and the exact entropy cancellations numerically.',
 'A vanishing summary statistic does not imply absent entanglement and cannot locate the claimed g=1 or1.67 transitions without a specified state and observable.',
 'Mixed three-party states can have nonzero I₃, and a subsystem of a larger pure system is generally mixed. The theorem is for a globally pure state of exactly A,B,C. No historical ψ(g) is reconstructed from endpoint labels.',
 'Specify a full normalized state family and compare reduced-spectrum invariants, genuine multipartite entanglement measures and filtration changes separately. Any proposed transition must be defined before numerical thresholds are tested.',
 [('Preskill quantum information and entropy notes','https://www.preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/chap10_6A_2022.pdf')])

add(8,'A filtration changes homology when simplices enter, not when labels sound singular','2025-05-18T02:28:11Z','U','root_receipts.json','U@2025-05-18T02:28:11Z',
 'What finite Hodge-Laplacian certificate distinguishes a weighted perturbation from a genuine filtration event?',
 'The historical state, weights and filtration were not recovered, so its claimed transition parameters cannot be recomputed. A completely declared triangle filtration nevertheless demonstrates the exact distinction needed: positive weights change eigenvalues, whereas adding a filling simplex can change homology. This is a new mathematical example, not an inferred ψ(g).',
 ['Orient triangle edges 01,12,20 and use boundary B₁=[[-1,0,1],[1,−1,0],[0,1,−1]]. Without a face, B₂ is absent; with a face its boundary column is (1,1,1)ᵀ.',
 'Use the unweighted edge Hodge Laplacian Δ₁=B₁ᵀB₁+B₂B₂ᵀ. A filtration is an increasing sequence of simplicial complexes, including all faces of every entered simplex.'],
 'The harmonic edge disappears exactly when the face is added',
 'For the unfilled triangle, Δ₁ has eigenvalues 0,3,3 with harmonic vector (1,1,1). After adding the face, Δ₁=3I and its kernel is zero. This matches β₁=1 and β₁=0 respectively.',
 'The boundary sum B₁(1,1,1)ᵀ is zero, so B₁B₂=0 and the complex is valid. Direct multiplication gives B₁ᵀB₁=3I−J, where J has every entry one. J has eigenvalue three on constants and zero on their orthogonal complement, producing the unfilled spectrum. Adding B₂B₂ᵀ=J yields 3I. More generally xᵀΔ₁x=||B₁x||²+||B₂ᵀx||²; therefore its kernel consists of cycles orthogonal to boundaries and represents homology in this finite inner-product model. Replacing the inner products by positive definite weights preserves the dimension of that harmonic space; positive weights do not by themselves create a new simplex or a new homology class.',
 'Let all edges enter at threshold zero and the face enter at threshold two.',
 'The degree-one persistence interval begins at zero and ends at two. Before two there is one independent edge cycle; afterwards it is the boundary of the new face. A weighted eigenvalue crossing zero caused only by a weight becoming singular would instead leave the positive-definite setting and requires a different explanation. The verifier checks both spectra through exact matrix identities and ranks.',
 'The certificate separates a genuine combinatorial event from an unproved wall-crossing interpretation of an unspecified entropy curve.',
 'A weighted hypergraph is not automatically a simplicial complex. Its boundary maps, orientations, filtration and inner products must be supplied before this Hodge argument applies. No historical transition value is validated.',
 'Given the actual state family, define each simplex’s entry time from an explicit entropy statistic, prove face monotonicity, and compute persistent homology with exact or interval-certified thresholds. Then compare those events with the quantum observables rather than identifying them by terminology.',
 [('MIT algebraic topology course materials','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-905-algebraic-topology-i-fall-2016/')])
add(9,'Tropicalization retains valuation constraints and forgets unit data','2025-05-21T03:29:31Z','A','root_receipts.json','A@2025-05-21T03:29:31Z',
 'What can the tropicalization of x+y+1=0 prove, and what information is lost before any proposed enriched decoder is added?',
 'A valuation map has a precise and useful loss of information. Its tropical balancing condition follows from the nonarchimedean triangle inequality, but it does not determine the original coordinates or a reconstruction advantage. The source’s additional filtered object needs an independently defined filtration.',
 ['Let K be a nonarchimedean valued field with additive valuation v, so v(xy)=v(x)+v(y) and v(x+y)≥min(v(x),v(y)). Set v(0)=∞.',
 'For nonzero x,y on X={x+y+1=0}, define trop(x,y)=(u,w)=(v(x),v(y)). Use the minimum convention.'],
 'A tropical line and a strict information-loss example',
 'Every such point satisfies that min(u,w,0) is attained at least twice. Its possible finite valuation pattern lies on the three rays (u,0) with u≥0, (0,w) with w≥0, or (u,u) with u≤0. Valuations alone do not test an individual proposed lift.',
 'If one of three nonzero summands has strictly smaller valuation than the other two, the sum of the other two has larger valuation and cannot cancel it. Therefore x+y+1=0 forces a repeated minimum. Enumerating which two entries attain it gives the three rays. Conversely, interpreting the rays as the complete tropical variety requires a lifting theorem over a suitable valued algebraic closure; only the elementary necessary condition is used here. The loss of unit data is concrete: over Q₃, (x,y)=(1,−2) lies on X and has valuations (0,0), while (1,1) has the same valuations but gives x+y+1=3≠0. Thus one valuation pair supports both an actual solution and a nonsolution depending on the leading units.',
 'Compare Q₃ points (1,−2), (3,−4) and the proposed pair (1,1).',
 'The first has valuation (0,0), the second (1,0); each has a repeated minimum and exactly satisfies the equation. The third has the same tropical coordinates as the first but fails the original equation. Multiplication also illustrates retained structure: v₃(3·(−4))=1+0=1. These exact integer valuations are checked without a p-adic numerical library.',
 'Tropicalization gives an exact coarse constraint, and the counterexample identifies the additional residue information that a reconstruction method would need.',
 'The source’s notation Trop₊(X)=(X^trop,F•) does not specify F• or prove a decoder. No detection threshold, resource gain or motivic comparison follows from adding a superscripted filtration symbol.',
 'Retain an initial-form equation over the residue field together with the tropical point. Prove which residue solutions lift under a stated nonsingularity condition; that gives a rigorous first refinement before attempting any higher filtered or motivic enhancement.',
 [('Sturmfels, tropical geometry and polynomial equations','https://math.berkeley.edu/~bernd/mathmag.pdf'),('Sturmfels, solving polynomial equations','https://math.berkeley.edu/~bernd/cbms.pdf')])
add(10,'A p-adic integral can be evaluated exactly by valuation shells','2025-05-21T03:29:31Z','A','root_receipts.json','A@2025-05-21T03:29:31Z',
 'Which measure and convergence region make the proposed p-adic integral meaningful, and what exact rational function results for |x|_p^s?',
 'The source names an integral over p-adic integer points, but an integrand, measure and convergence claim still have to be specified. The monomial case supplies a complete local-zeta calculation: its rationality comes from a geometric series rather than from a speculative decoding interpretation.',
 ['Fix a prime p and normalize Haar measure μ(Z_p)=1. Then μ(p^nZ_p)=p^(−n), and |x|_p=p^(−v_p(x)) for x≠0.',
 'For complex s define |x|_p^s=exp(s log|x|_p). The singleton zero has measure zero and may be assigned any value when the integral is finite.'],
 'An exact local zeta integral',
 'For Re(s)>−1, Z_p(s)=∫_{Z_p}|x|_p^s dμ=(1−p^(−1))/(1−p^(−s−1)). The integral is absolutely convergent exactly in this half-plane; the displayed rational-in-p^(−s) expression also supplies a meromorphic continuation.',
 'Partition nonzero Z_p into shells A_n=p^nZ_p∖p^(n+1)Z_p, n≥0. Their measures are (1−p^(−1))p^(−n), and the integrand is constant p^(−ns) on each shell. Thus Z_p(s)=(1−p^(−1))Σ_{n≥0}p^(−n(s+1)). Absolute convergence is equivalent to |p^(−s−1)|<1, or Re(s)>−1, and summation gives the formula. If Re(s)≤−1, the positive integral of the absolute value diverges by the same series. Analytic continuation is a statement about the resulting function; it does not make the original divergent integral convergent outside its initial half-plane.',
 'Choose p=3 and s=2, then compare shell integration with a finite residue approximation.',
 'The exact answer is (2/3)/(1−1/27)=9/13. The first N shells sum to (9/13)(1−27^(−N)), with omitted tail (9/13)27^(−N). In a residue calculation modulo 3^N, each congruence class has measure 3^(−N); valuations below N reproduce those shell counts exactly. The unresolved zero class contributes a bounded tail rather than an arbitrary replacement value.',
 'The calculation includes its normalization, domain and an exact truncation error, making it suitable for a reproducible arithmetic implementation.',
 'A general variety X(Z_p), singular polynomial or oscillatory integrand needs additional geometry and convergence hypotheses. This one-variable proof is not a proof of the full rationality theory or of a physical detector.',
 'For a polynomial f, count solutions f(x)≡0 modulo p^N and relate those counts to the measure of valuation sublevel sets. A proved resolution or cell decomposition can then replace the elementary shell structure used here.',
 [('Igusa, theory of local zeta functions','https://doi.org/10.1090/amsip/014'),('Denef, report on Igusa local zeta functions','https://www.numdam.org/book-part/SB_1990-1991__33__359_0/')])
add(11,'Derived tensor products record information an ordinary product erases','2025-05-21T01:13:59Z','A','root_receipts.json','A@2025-05-21T01:13:59Z',
 'What concrete higher algebraic information justifies using a derived construction before speaking about derived motivic stacks?',
 'The recovered manuscript description lists derived objects but supplies no defining equations. A small algebraic example shows exactly why deriving a functor can matter. It is deliberately an authored comparison, not a claimed construction of the absent stacks, sheaves or path integrals.',
 ['Let k be a field, A=k[ε]/(ε²), and regard k=A/(ε) as an A-module. Ordinary tensor gives k⊗_Ak≅k.',
 'Tor_n^A(k,k) is the nth homology of a free resolution of one copy of k after tensoring with the other. The derived tensor k⊗_A^Lk retains this whole homology sequence.'],
 'An explicit resolution with a higher class in every degree',
 'For every n≥0, Tor_n^A(k,k)≅k. Thus the derived tensor cannot be replaced by its degree-zero ordinary tensor without losing nonzero information in every positive degree.',
 'Use the augmented complex …→A→A→A→k→0, with every positive-degree differential multiplication by ε and the final map the quotient. Consecutive differentials compose to multiplication by ε²=0. In A, an element is a+bε; multiplying by ε gives aε, so the kernel and image of multiplication by ε are both the one-dimensional ideal εA. The kernel of A→k is also εA. Hence the complex is an exact free resolution. Tensoring with k sends ε to zero, so all resulting differentials vanish and each chain group is k. Its homology is therefore k in every nonnegative degree. This is a complete calculation rather than an invocation of derived language alone.',
 'Represent multiplication by ε on the basis (1,ε) as M=[[0,0],[1,0]].',
 'Then M²=0, rank(M)=1 and kernel(M)=span(ε)=image(M). Reducing coefficients modulo ε turns M into zero on the tensor complex. The verifier checks these finite linear identities and the first eight homology dimensions. The written periodic argument proves the all-degree statement.',
 'Higher Tor is the information that ordinary tensor discards; a derived replacement must be justified by such an actual failure of exactness.',
 'This is a module calculation over dual numbers, not a motivic cohomology computation, an E∞ structure or a syntomic integration measure. Passing from complexes to those theories requires their own objects, maps and comparison theorems.',
 'Build a small verified chain-complex library that certifies d²=0, exactness and chain-homotopy equivalence. Only then add a geometric input functor and ask which derived invariants it computes, with explicit test objects and coefficient rings.',
 [('Stacks Project homological algebra','https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/010V'),('de Jong commutative algebra course and Tor constructions','https://www.math.columbia.edu/~dejong/courses/commutative-algebra-fall-2021.html')])
add(12,'Energy ratios require a shared boundary and a complete denominator','2025-05-22T03:48:58Z','A','root_receipts.json','A@2025-05-22T03:48:58Z',
 'Which arithmetic conclusions follow from the stated output and EROI numbers, and what information is missing before any performance improvement can be claimed?',
 'The recovered energy report contains mutually inconsistent ratios and system boundaries. The mathematical repair is an accounting model, with energies measured over the same interval and with the same conversion convention. No harvesting performance or hardware design is inferred.',
 ['For a stipulated system boundary let E_out≥0 be useful returned energy and E_in>0 invested energy in the same units and time horizon. Define R=E_out/E_in.',
 'For a rated power P_max>0 over duration T, define capacity factor CF=E_out/(P_maxT), assuming output never exceeds that rating. A duty fraction measures time on, which is a different quantity.'],
 'Ratio audit, monotonicity and a duty-factor bound',
 'An output of11923kWh with ratio28.6 requires an invested denominator11923/28.6≈416.8881119kWh. The output alone does not determine EROI. If output is zero when off and at most P_max when on, then0≤CF≤duty fraction, with equality only at rated output almost everywhere during on-time.',
 'The implied denominator follows by rearranging R=E_out/E_in; no engineering hypothesis is hidden in that arithmetic. For a time-varying output P(t), energy is ∫₀ᵀP(t)dt. If the on-set has measure dT and 0≤P≤P_max there, then the integral is at most dTP_max, proving CF≤d. The ratio R increases with E_out and decreases with E_in, so independent positive interval inputs yield the sharp rectangle enclosure [E_out,min/E_in,max,E_out,max/E_in,min]. Adding an omitted positive investment strictly decreases the ratio. Converting a thermal output to an electrical equivalent with factor η≤1 multiplies that output contribution by η; it cannot be left numerically unchanged while the denominator is changed.',
 'Use a constructed two-hour schedule with rated power ten units, on for one hour at half rating, and off otherwise.',
 'Duty fraction is1/2, energy is five units and CF=5/(10·2)=1/4. Calling a60% duty assumption a60% capacity factor would therefore require an additional rated-output assumption. For the source’s reported output, the denominator implied by35:1 would be11923/35≈340.6571kWh, different from the denominator implied by28.6:1; neither is recovered as an actual measured input.',
 'The audit identifies what can be solved algebraically and what remains an unsupported model or measurement claim.',
 'Ratios0.28 thermal,5.67 electrical and above35 cannot be compared without explicit boundary and conversion definitions. This mathematical treatment supplies no lifecycle estimate, cost forecast or physical validation.',
 'Represent a complete accounting boundary as a directed flow graph with tagged units and time horizons. Prove conservation at every node and generate ratio intervals only after excluding double-counted internal transfers.',
 [('NIST SI energy and power unit conventions','https://www.nist.gov/pml/special-publication-811/nist-guide-si-chapter-4-two-classes-si-units-and-si-prefixes')])
add(13,'The stated gravity-wave dispersion has perpendicular phase and group directions','2025-05-22T04:04:37Z','A','root_receipts.json','A@2025-05-22T04:04:37Z',
 'For the stated linear dispersion relation, what frequency and group velocity are actually implied, and does that establish recoverable power?',
 'A dispersion relation can support an exact propagation calculation without supporting the reported energy gains. The nonrotating constant-stratification model has a particularly clear geometry: its frequency is homogeneous of degree zero in wavevector, forcing phase and group directions to be perpendicular.',
 ['Take a stipulated N>0, horizontal wavenumber k>0 and vertical wavenumber m∈R. Use ω(k,m)=Nk/√(k²+m²), the positive branch of ω²=N²k²/(k²+m²).',
 'Define group velocity c_g=∇_(k,m)ω and phase-velocity vector c_ph=ω(k,m)(k,m)/(k²+m²). This is the stated ideal linear nonrotating dispersion model.'],
 'An exact group-velocity formula and orthogonality',
 'c_g=(Nm²,−Nkm)/(k²+m²)^(3/2), and (k,m)·c_g=0. Hence c_ph·c_g=0. Also0<ω≤N, with equality only when m=0.',
 'Differentiate Nk(k²+m²)^(−1/2) in k to obtain N[(k²+m²)−k²]/(k²+m²)^(3/2)=Nm²/(k²+m²)^(3/2). Differentiating in m gives −Nkm/(k²+m²)^(3/2). Taking the wavevector dot product cancels the two numerator terms. Equivalently ω(sk,sm)=ω(k,m) for s>0, so Euler’s homogeneous-function identity forces that dot product to vanish. The frequency bound follows from k≤√(k²+m²). At m=0 the group velocity vanishes in this idealized expression; this limiting degeneracy must not be mistaken for a general transport prediction.',
 'Use nondimensional values N=5,k=3,m=4.',
 'Then ω=3, c_g=(16/25,−12/25), and c_ph=(9/25,12/25). Their dot product is144/625−144/625=0. The wavevector dot product is48/25−48/25=0. If both wavenumbers are doubled to(6,8), the frequency stays three while both group-velocity components are halved. This follows from differentiating a degree-zero homogeneous function and provides a second scaling check. The verifier checks the displayed fractions exactly; the all-parameter derivative proof supplies the general statement.',
 'The geometry follows from a specified equation and can be certified independently of the harvesting proposal.',
 'Wave amplitude, energy density, dissipation, forcing and coupling are absent from a dispersion equation. It cannot determine watts per patch, total output or EROI. Rotation, variable stratification and boundaries require a different model.',
 'Supply a linear wave-energy density and boundary coupling operator, then prove an input-output energy inequality. Such an inequality would be the proper mathematical bridge from propagation to an upper bound on extractable energy.',
 [('MIT internal gravity-wave notes','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-810-dynamics-of-the-atmosphere-spring-2023/resources/mit12_810_s23_intro_igw_pdf/'),('MIT WKB internal-wave lectures','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-802-wave-motion-in-the-ocean-and-the-atmosphere-spring-2008/pages/lecture-notes/')])
add(14,'A tolerance relation cannot serve as field equality','2025-05-23T23:36:13Z','U','root_details.json','infra-rational is',
 'Can contextual numerical closeness be equality in a field, and what happens if transitivity is imposed after the fact?',
 'The user’s infra-rational proposal explicitly allows non-transitive equality. That can be a useful tolerance relation, but it is incompatible with algebraic equality. The obstruction is elementary and remains even before multiplication, contexts or uncertainty propagation are introduced.',
 ['Fix δ>0 rational and define x∼y on Q by |x−y|<δ. A field’s equality relation must be reflexive, symmetric and transitive and must respect both operations.',
 'The transitive closure of a relation declares two points equivalent whenever a finite chain of related points connects them.'],
 'Nontransitivity and collapse of the naive quotient',
 'The tolerance relation is not transitive. Its transitive closure on Q is the universal relation, so quotienting by that closure identifies every rational number, including zero and one.',
 'Take x=0,y=3δ/4,z=3δ/2. Both neighboring differences are3δ/4<δ, but |x−z|=3δ/2>δ. Thus the relation cannot be substituted for equality in a field. For any two rational a,b, choose an integer N>|b−a|/δ. The rational chain a+j(b−a)/N, j=0,…,N, has every step smaller thanδ. Transitive closure therefore equates a and b. A quotient with0=1 is not a field under the usual nontrivial-field axiom. Merely closing the relation under transitivity does not repair the intended numerical information; it erases it.',
 'Set δ=1 and use the explicit triple0,3/4,3/2.',
 'The two true closeness tests and one false endpoint test are exact rational comparisons. A chain from zero to ten with twenty equal steps also shows why iterated tolerances can travel arbitrarily far. The verifier checks these examples and constructs chains between several rational endpoints.',
 'Tolerance should be a separately named relation whose inferential rules are controlled; it cannot inherit equality substitution automatically.',
 'This is a counterexample to a fixed positive tolerance. A context-dependent threshold may produce other relations, but a proposed quotient still needs an actual equivalence relation and congruence proof. At zero threshold the source’s strict inequality is not even reflexive.',
 'Use exact equality for values and store error bounds as additional data, or choose a declared rounding map q and define x≈y iff q(x)=q(y). The latter is an equivalence, but its arithmetic compatibility must still be tested rather than assumed.',
 [('MIT algebra quotient and field foundations','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-701-algebra-i-fall-2010/')])
add(15,'Uncertainty propagation needs covariance and does not create a field','2025-05-23T23:36:13Z','U','root_details.json','multiplication uncertainty',
 'When is quadrature addition exact, what term is missing from the proposed multiplication uncertainty, and why does x−x expose lost provenance?',
 'The proposed uncertainties resemble standard deviations of independent random errors. They are not universal interval laws, and ignoring dependence destroys algebraic cancellations. An exact independent-product variance calculation identifies a further term omitted by first-order propagation.',
 ['Let X,Y have means v,w and variances ε²,η². For independent variables assume finite second moments. For dependent variables write Cov(X,Y).',
 'A stored pair (mean,standard deviation) does not record the joint law or whether two appearances refer to the same random variable.'],
 'Exact variance identities and a missing second-order term',
 'Var(X+Y)=ε²+η²+2Cov(X,Y). Under independence, Var(XY)=v²η²+w²ε²+ε²η². The source’s relative-error product formula retains only the first two terms and is undefined as written at zero means.',
 'Expand the centered square of X+Y and take expectations to obtain the covariance identity. For independent X,Y, E[X²Y²]=E[X²]E[Y²]=(v²+ε²)(w²+η²), while E[XY]=vw. Subtracting v²w² gives the exact product variance. The formula |vw|√[(ε/v)²+(η/w)²] has squared value w²ε²+v²η² when v,w≠0, omitting the nonnegative product term. For cancellation, using Y=−X gives covariance −ε² and exact zero variance. Treating those two appearances as independent instead reports2ε², demonstrating the lost dependence information.',
 'Take independent Rademacher errors Z,W with values±1 equally likely and X=1+Z,Y=1+W.',
 'The product takes values0,0,0,4 with equal probability. Its mean is one, second moment four and variance three. The first-order formula gives variance two. For the same X, X−X is exactly zero, whereas blind quadrature reports standard deviation√2. The verifier enumerates all four outcomes exactly.',
 'The repaired formulas explain the approximation and preserve the difference between independent measurements and repeated use of one uncertain quantity.',
 'Means and variances alone do not define a field. The random variable1+Z is nonzero as an algebra element yet vanishes on an event of probability1/2, so it has no pointwise multiplicative inverse. Context labels cannot remove that obstruction without changing the algebra.',
 'Use an explicit affine-error model X=v+Σa_jZ_j with shared noise symbols to preserve correlations under addition, and retain higher-order symbols or rigorously bounded remainders under multiplication. Then prove enclosure or moment bounds for each operation.',
 [('NIST uncertainty propagation guidelines','https://www.nist.gov/pml/nist-technical-note-1297/nist-tn-1297-5-combined-standard-uncertainty')])
add(16,'Contextual arithmetic needs restriction maps before sheaf claims','2025-05-23T23:52:46Z','A','root_receipts.json','A@2025-05-23T23:52:46Z',
 'What simple context model really satisfies a gluing theorem, and which data are missing from the proposed fibred or sheaf semantics?',
 'The source’s categorical expansion is truncated after its fibred-category claim. A context poset, compatible operations and a collapse symbol do not alone define a sheaf or fibration. A finite observation-domain model supplies a genuine gluing statement while making the missing morphisms visible.',
 ['Fix a finite set Ω. Contexts are subsets U⊆Ω, ordered by inclusion. Define F(U)=Q^U, the rational-valued functions on U.',
 'For V⊆U, restriction res^U_V sends f to f|_V. A cover of U is a family U_i with union U. Compatibility means equal restrictions on every overlap U_i∩U_j.'],
 'A concrete sheaf of contextual values',
 'Every compatible family f_i∈F(U_i) glues to a unique f∈F(U). Restrictions preserve pointwise addition and multiplication. Thus this model has exact contextual algebra without replacing equality by numerical tolerance.',
 'Define f(ω)=f_i(ω) using any i withω∈U_i. Compatibility on overlaps makes the value independent of the choice. Each restriction equals the prescribed f_i. If another function has those same restrictions, its value at anyω is fixed by one covering set, proving uniqueness. Restriction identities res^U_U=id and res^V_W∘res^U_V=res^U_W follow directly from evaluating functions. Pointwise arithmetic commutes with restriction because both sides evaluate to the same rational sum or product. These are actual maps and equations; they are the data absent from a bare statement that contexts compose by a semilattice join.',
 'Take Ω={a,b,c}, U₁={a,b}, U₂={b,c}, with f₁(a)=1,f₁(b)=2 and f₂(b)=2,f₂(c)=3.',
 'The unique glued function is(1,2,3). Changing f₂(b) to5 destroys compatibility, and no global function can have both restrictions. Pointwise functions form a product ring Q³, not a field: the nonzero functions(1,0,0) and(0,1,0) multiply to zero. The verifier checks gluing and this zero-divisor certificate exactly.',
 'A genuine categorical context model can preserve local arithmetic while still lacking a global field structure.',
 'This authored finite sheaf is not a reconstruction of the source’s unknown category IRat, its morphisms or its claimed topos. Units, provenance and error intervals would need additional well-typed structures; incompatible values cannot simply be glued by notation.',
 'Build a sheaf of sets of admissible values or a stack of uncertainty models with explicit descent data. Prove how provenance and shared random variables restrict and glue before asserting equivalence with the original numerical proposal.',
 [('Stacks Project sheaves and gluing','https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/006S')])


add(17,'Totient-weighted variation is a hybrid optimization problem','2025-05-24T00:26:43Z','U','root_details.json','Definitions:',
 'When does the weight φ(n)/σ(n) change Euler–Lagrange dynamics, and what junction law is required at a prescribed discrete mode switch?',
 'The user explicitly restricted this proposal to grounded hybrid control and optimization. For a prescribed integer mode, the action weight is a positive arithmetic scalar. It cancels from the interior Euler–Lagrange equation when constant, but it matters at a switch and in the globally optimal allocation of velocity.',
 ['Let n(t) be a prescribed piecewise constant positive integer with finitely many fixed switch times. Put w(t)=φ(n(t))/σ(n(t))>0.',
 'For a continuous piecewise C² path q, use S[q]=∫w(t)L(q,qdot,t)dt with fixed endpoints. Variations are continuous at switches; no jump penalty is included.'],
 'Interior equation and weighted momentum continuity',
 'On each constant-mode interval, d/dt(L_v)−L_q=0. At a fixed switch t₀, a stationary path must satisfy w_-L_v(q,qdot_-,t₀)=w_+L_v(q,qdot_+,t₀). Differentiating φ at an integer is neither needed nor defined by this model.',
 'Take the first variation ∫w(L_qδq+L_vδqdot)dt separately on every interval. Integration by parts gives the interior factor w[L_q−d(L_v)/dt], which vanishes because w is constant and positive. Boundary terms at a shared fixed switch combine as (w_-L_v^-−w_+L_v^+)δq(t₀). Arbitrary continuous variation there gives the momentum condition. For L=v²/2, the entire optimization also follows from Cauchy–Schwarz: (q(T)−q(0))²=(∫sqrt(w)v·w^(−1/2)dt)²≤(∫wv²dt)(∫w^(−1)dt). Equality requires wv constant, so S_min=(Δq)²/[2∫w^(−1)dt]. This is a global certificate for the stated convex problem.',
 'Set T=1, q(0)=0,q(1)=1; choose n=1 on the first half and n=2 on the second.',
 'The weights are1 and1/3. Since ∫w^(−1)dt=2, the minimum action is1/4. The optimal velocities are1/2 and3/2, with weighted momentum1/2 on both halves. A constant velocity instead gives action1/3. The verifier checks the endpoint, junction and objective values with exact rational arithmetic.',
 'The arithmetic weight influences the optimization through declared mode choices and switch conditions, without introducing speculative physical laws.',
 'If switch times are free, additional transversality conditions arise. If n depends on the path or jumps by feedback, guards, resets and admissible controls must be specified. The source’s extra forcing term ΣR_ijλ_j requires its own constraint or forcing derivation.',
 'Optimize over a finite mode set with switching penalties. Derive a Bellman recursion for the discrete choices and a convex continuous subproblem within each schedule, then prove a lower bound against which a proposed algorithm can be certified.',
 [('Liberzon calculus of variations and optimal control','https://liberzon.csl.illinois.edu/teaching/cvoc/'),('MIT theory of numbers','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-781-theory-of-numbers-spring-2012/pages/lecture-notes/')])
add(18,'The recovered gcd coupling defines a valid graph, not an automatic rigidity law','2025-05-24T00:26:43Z','U','root_details.json','Definitions:',
 'What can be proved from the actual R_ij formula and coprime set, and how should an integer arithmetic state evolve in a hybrid model?',
 'The retrieved coupling is explicit enough for a graph theorem. Its nonnegative symmetric entries yield a dissipative graph Laplacian regardless of whether the coupling matrix itself is positive definite. A separate elementary obstruction shows why a continuously differentiable integer state cannot satisfy an arbitrary nonzero velocity law.',
 ['On a finite set of distinct positive labels define R_ij=φ(gcd(i,j))/max(i,j) when gcd(i,j)>1, and zero otherwise. Define L=diag(R1)−R.',
 'For m≥1, the coprime residue set is E_m={k mod m:gcd(k,m)=1}, of sizeφ(m). The unrestricted positive-integer set of coprime k is infinite.',
 'The Möbius function μ is zero on integers with squared prime factors and equals(−1)^r on a product of r distinct primes.'],
 'A graph energy identity and an integer-state obstruction',
 'L is positive semidefinite and xᵀLx=(1/2)Σ_ijR_ij(x_i−x_j)². Its kernel is constant on each connected component of the positive-weight graph. Any continuous n:I→Z on an interval is constant, so a nonzero formula for ndot requires a real relaxation or a jump model.',
 'The formula makes R symmetric and nonnegative. Expanding the square and using symmetry proves the energy identity; it vanishes precisely when coordinates agree along every positive edge, hence along each connected component. For the integer-state claim, continuity sends a connected interval to a connected subset of Z, and every connected subset of Z is a singleton. Thus an equation ndot=αΣ||qdot_i||²μ(d_i) cannot simultaneously describe an integer-valued differentiable n unless its right-hand side vanishes. The Möbius sign can be used as a specified discrete update, but its arithmetic definition alone does not produce a dynamical theorem. A valid arithmetic identity is Σ_{d|m}φ(d)=m, whose Dirichlet Möbius inversion is φ=μ*id; it should not be confused with time evolution.',
 'Use labels2,3,6 and ignore self-loops in the graph Laplacian.',
 'The only off-diagonal weights are R_26=1/6 and R_36=1/3. The Laplacian has characteristic polynomial λ(λ²−λ+1/6), so its positive eigenvalues are(3±√3)/6. Coprime labels2 and3 have no direct edge but are linked through6. Modulo6 the coprime residue set is{1,5}, of sizeφ(6)=2.',
 'The exact graph certificate supplies a meaningful network model while preserving the distinction between connectivity, controllability and the proposed integer update.',
 'A graph gap is not a complete controllability criterion for an input pair(A,B). Mechanical rigidity and energy-barrier claims need their own definitions. The user’s explicit exclusion of speculative physics is retained.',
 'Choose input vertices and form a declared linear control system on the mean-zero graph space. Compute its Kalman rank or controllability Gramian exactly, then study how the arithmetic label set changes that certificate.',
 [('MIT totient and divisor identities','https://math.mit.edu/~dav/781ps4sol.pdf'),('Stanley, Enumerative Combinatorics and Möbius inversion','https://math.mit.edu/~rstan/ec/ec1.pdf')])
add(19,'Finite spectral zeta and heat transforms do not imply a universal density law','2025-05-24T04:27:42Z','U','root_receipts.json','U@2025-05-24T04:27:42Z',
 'Which exact identities connect a finite positive spectrum, its heat trace and spectral zeta, and what makes the claimed small-eigenvalue density nonnormalizable without a cutoff?',
 'The report combines a learned spectral fit with a zeta-like series and transform inversion. These are different levels of claim. A finite positive spectrum gives exact analytic formulas; an asymptotic law requires a specified operator family, limiting measure and normalization.',
 ['Let0<λ₁,…,λ_r be positive eigenvalues counted with multiplicity. Define heat trace H(t)=Σe^(−tλ_i), t>0, and ζ_A(s)=Σλ_i^(−s). Exclude zero eigenvalues explicitly.',
 'A proposed density ρ(λ)=Cλ^(−α) on(0,b] is integrable near zero only ifα<1. A finite lower cutoff changes this condition.'],
 'A finite Mellin identity and an integrability obstruction',
 'For Re(s)>0, Γ(s)ζ_A(s)=∫₀∞t^(s−1)H(t)dt. The finite spectral zeta itself is entire in s. A nonzero density proportional toλ^(−1.528) cannot be normalized on(0,b] without a cutoff or another modification.',
 'For one positive λ, substitute u=tλ in the gamma integral to obtain ∫₀∞t^(s−1)e^(−tλ)dt=λ^(−s)Γ(s). A finite sum can be interchanged with the integral, proving the identity. Each λ^(−s)=exp(−slogλ) is entire, so the finite sum is entire; the integral representation has its own restricted convergence region. For the density claim, ∫_ερ(λ)dλ=C[b^(1−α)−ε^(1−α)]/(1−α) whenα≠1. Ifα>1 this diverges asε→0. Therefore the fitted exponent does not define a probability density all the way to zero. Neither a finite fit nor this transform identity establishes a limiting law for an undefined arithmetic graph.',
 'Use the positive path-three Laplacian spectrum{1,3}.',
 'H(t)=e^(−t)+e^(−3t), ζ_A(1)=4/3 and ζ_A(2)=10/9. Direct integration gives∫H=4/3 and∫tH=10/9 becauseΓ(1)=Γ(2)=1. For a cutoff density on[a,b], the normalization constant is(1−α)/(b^(1−α)−a^(1−α)), which is positive forα>1 as well; the dependence on a cannot be omitted.',
 'The exact transform statements remain useful after the universal spectral and number-theoretic interpretations are removed.',
 'An infinite spectrum requires convergence estimates before summing, integrating or analytically continuing. No λ₁ asymptotic, Riemann-type zero theorem or prime-gap prediction is obtained from a finite spectral definition.',
 'Specify a sequence of graph operators and empirical spectral measures. Prove tightness and identify any limiting density on a stated scaling window, keeping the smallest eigenvalue and zero-mode mass separate.',
 [('NIST DLMF gamma integral','https://dlmf.nist.gov/5.2'),('NIST DLMF Mellin transforms','https://dlmf.nist.gov/2.5')])
add(20,'Multiplicative convolution has a precise divisor algebra','2025-05-24T04:44:21Z','U','root_receipts.json','U@2025-05-24T04:44:21Z',
 'Which multiplicative convolution is intended, when do transforms turn it into a product, and what exact arithmetic identity can an implementation certify?',
 'The historical report’s multiplicative-function approximation and inversion claims lack their actual kernel and norms. The classical divisor convolution gives a well-defined replacement. It proves an exact transform product when absolute convergence permits regrouping, rather than asserting a universal approximation capability.',
 ['For arithmetic functions f,g:N→C, define(f*g)(n)=Σ_{d|n}f(d)g(n/d). Let1(n)=1 and ε(1)=1, ε(n)=0 for n>1.',
 'A function is multiplicative if f(mn)=f(m)f(n) for coprime m,n and f(1)=1. Its Dirichlet series is D_f(s)=Σf(n)n^(−s) where absolutely convergent.'],
 'Convolution, inversion and a transform product',
 'Dirichlet convolution is associative and commutative with identity ε. The Möbius function satisfies μ*1=ε. Whenever D_f,D_g converge absolutely, D_(f*g)(s)=D_f(s)D_g(s). Multiplicative inputs give a multiplicative convolution.',
 'Associativity follows by writing either parenthesization as the finite sum overabc=n of f(a)g(b)h(c); commutativity swaps complementary divisors. For μ, summing μ(d) over divisors of n factors as∏_{p|n}(1−1), zero unless n=1. The Dirichlet-series product is the absolutely convergent double sumΣ_{a,b}f(a)g(b)(ab)^(−s); group terms by n=ab to obtain the formula. For coprime m,n, each divisor of mn decomposes uniquely into a divisor of m times a divisor of n, so the convolution sum factors. These are concrete algebraic and analytic hypotheses, not consequences of a neural architecture name.',
 'Take f=g=1, then invert the divisor-count function τ=1*1 by μ.',
 'τ(12)=6 from divisors1,2,3,4,6,12. The identity μ*τ=1 gives at12 the sum τ(12)−τ(6)−τ(4)+τ(2)=6−4−3+2=1. Consequently the absolutely convergent series of τ is ζ(s)² for Re(s)>1. The verifier checks the convolution and inversion on every integer through120.',
 'A finite implementation can certify these exact arithmetic identities while leaving approximation and extrapolation claims separate.',
 'This does not prove exponential approximation of arbitrary multiplicative functions, inversion of a noisy learned transform, or a number-theoretic theorem from finite training. Other notions of multiplicative convolution, such as Haar convolution on R_+, need different definitions.',
 'For a specified truncated divisor transform, compute its triangular matrix and exact inverse. Then derive a perturbation norm bound for noisy coefficients, making conditioning and truncation error explicit before claiming stable inversion.',
 [('NIST DLMF Dirichlet series','https://dlmf.nist.gov/27.4'),('MIT number-theory lecture notes','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-781-theory-of-numbers-spring-2012/pages/lecture-notes/')])
add(21,'A PAC–Bayes guarantee needs a sampling law, prior and bounded loss','2025-05-24T04:27:42Z','U','root_receipts.json','U@2025-05-24T04:27:42Z',
 'What actual assumptions produce a generalization bound, and why does a reported prime-gap R²=.847 not certify one?',
 'A fitted score and a theorem about unseen samples are different mathematical objects. The source’s spectral PAC–Bayes claim does not provide its prior, posterior, sample law or loss bound. A standard finite-hypothesis theorem can be derived transparently and shows exactly where those assumptions enter.',
 ['Let H be finite, P a strictly positive prior fixed independently of an iid sample of size m, and ℓ(h,z)∈[0,1]. Define population loss L(h), empirical loss Lhat(h), and KL(Q||P)=ΣQ(h)log[Q(h)/P(h)].',
 'Fix λ>0 andδ∈(0,1) before seeing the sample. Write L(Q),Lhat(Q) for posterior averages.'],
 'A fixed-parameter PAC–Bayes–Hoeffding bound',
 'With probability at least1−δ, simultaneously for every posterior Q, L(Q)≤Lhat(Q)+[KL(Q||P)+log(1/δ)]/λ+λ/(8m). This is a theorem under the declared iid bounded-loss model, not a consequence of R² or a spectral fit.',
 'Hoeffding’s bounded-variable lemma gives E_sample exp(λ[L(h)−Lhat(h)])≤exp(λ²/(8m)) for each h. Average over the fixed prior and apply Markov: with probability at least1−δ, log E_P exp(λ[L−Lhat])≤λ²/(8m)+log(1/δ). For any finite probability Q, Jensen’s inequality applied after writing the prior sum using Q gives λE_Q(L−Lhat)≤KL(Q||P)+log E_P exp(λ[L−Lhat]). Combining proves the bound uniformly in Q on that one event. Zero posterior masses are handled by continuity. Optimizing λ after observing Q requires a separate uniformization or parameter grid; the fixed-λ theorem does not silently supply it.',
 'Choose two hypotheses with uniform prior, a point-mass posterior, m=100,δ=.05,λ=20 and empirical loss.1.',
 'Then KL=log2 and the penalty is log40/20+20/800≈.2094439727, giving L(Q)≤.3094439727 on the stated event. Separately R²=1−SSE/SST only describes squared errors on its evaluated response set; the number.847 omits the response normalization, split and dependency assumptions needed to connect it with this theorem.',
 'The proof makes its probability event, optimization domain and constants reviewable, while preserving the empirical report as a claim rather than a verified forecast.',
 'Prime-indexed observations are not automatically iid. Unbounded squared loss is outside the bounded-loss theorem unless explicitly controlled. A data-dependent prior needs another theorem. No cryptographic or cross-domain benefit follows from this certificate.',
 'Specify a dependency model, bounded or tail-controlled loss and a prior independent of the held-out data. A spectral hypothesis can enter only through a proved complexity or KL estimate, after which the actual numerical bound should be reported alongside the score.',
 [('Seldin et al., PAC–Bayesian inequalities for martingales','https://arxiv.org/abs/1110.6886'),('Seldin PAC–Bayesian thesis','https://people.kyb.tuebingen.mpg.de/seldin/publications/SeldinPhDThesis.pdf')])


add(22,'A thermal pulse bound reverses the source area-to-volume factor','2025-05-30T02:40:47Z','A','root_late_month.json','A@2025-05-30T02:40:47Z',
 'What is the dimensionally correct thermal-buffering flux bound, and what fraction of a first-order response occurs during a0.2ms pulse when the time constant is0.8s?',
 'The late-month source includes a heat-flux formula with A/V where conservation requires V/A. A short energy balance fixes that inversion. A separate first-order response calculation gives an exact scale comparison without interpreting the time-scale mismatch as measured device behavior.',
 ['For a uniform thermal capacity C=ρc_pV, a permitted temperature rise ΔT stores energy CΔT. Let A be the area receiving a uniform flux q″ for duration t_p.',
 'For a stated lumped linear model, τTdot+T=T_input after normalizing the temperature deviation. A unit step starting from zero gives T(t)=1−e^(−t/τ).'],
 'Energy-limited flux and a certified short-pulse response',
 'Ignoring heat losses, q″≤ρc_pΔT(V/A)/t_p is necessary to keep the stored-energy rise within CΔT. The source’s factor(A/V) has the wrong length dimension. With τ=.8s and t_p=.0002s, the step fraction is1−e^(−1/4000)≈.000249968753.',
 'Incoming energy is q″At_p. Requiring it to be no more thanρc_pVΔT and dividing by positive At_p gives the bound. The units are[J/(m³K)]·K·m/s=W/m²; replacing V/A by A/V would instead give W/m⁴. For the response, solve τTdot+T=1 by an integrating factor to obtain1−e^(−t/τ). For0≤x≤1, integrating1−u≤e^(−u)≤1 from zero to x gives x−x²/2≤1−e^(−x)≤x. Taking x=1/4000 encloses the response between.00024996875 and.00025. This is a rigorous bound in the declared first-order model.',
 'Use a constructed normalized capacity densityρc_p=2, volume V=3, area A=6,ΔT=4 and pulse duration2.',
 'Stored capacity is24 energy units and the allowed uniform flux is24/(6·2)=2. The source’s inverted geometry would produce8, four times larger in this example and with inconsistent dimensions. Separately τ/t_p=4000 for the retrieved time scales, so a full response within one pulse is not supplied by this single-state step model.',
 'The conservation and exponential calculations are practical mathematical checks, independent of unvalidated performance percentages.',
 'Spatial gradients, contact losses, phase changes and pulse trains require a PDE or additional states. The bound is necessary within its energy accounting, not a validated heat-load rating. The user’s rejection of impractical cohomological deployment claims is preserved.',
 'For a nonnegative pulse train convolved with the exponential impulse response, derive worst-case temperature envelopes from pulse energy and spacing. Validate the identified time constant against measurements before using the envelope for any design conclusion.',
 [('MIT heat-equation and transport course','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-51-intermediate-heat-and-mass-transfer-fall-2011/'),('NIST SI derived units','https://www.nist.gov/pml/special-publication-811/nist-guide-si-chapter-4-two-classes-si-units-and-si-prefixes')])
add(23,'Effective conductivity depends on geometry and interface resistance','2025-05-31T17:23:55Z','U','root_late_month.json','U@2025-05-31T17:23:55Z',
 'How do Fourier flux, series and parallel composites differ, and why do a filler fraction and two conductivities not determine one effective conductivity?',
 'Practical vector calculus is enough to expose the missing geometry. Parallel layers and series layers made from identical fractions have different exact effective conductivities. An interface resistance adds another independent parameter, so neither a nominal filler percentage nor a single multiplication validates the reported heat flux.',
 ['Use Fourier’s law q=−κ∇T withκ>0. On a stationary one-dimensional layered sample, constant flux and temperature drops determine its effective conductivity.',
 'Let fractions f and1−f have conductivitiesκ₂ andκ₁. Parallel paths share a gradient; series layers share a flux. An interface resistance R_c≥0 gives temperature jump R_cq″.'],
 'Two exact effective-conductivity formulas and a conservation identity',
 'Parallel geometry givesκ_parallel=(1−f)κ₁+fκ₂; series geometry givesκ_series=[(1−f)/κ₁+f/κ₂]^(−1). For total thicknessℓ, contact resistance changes the series value toκ_eff=ℓ/[ℓ₁/κ₁+ℓ₂/κ₂+R_c]. Heat conservation isρc_pT_t=−div q+s.',
 'In parallel, sum area-weighted fluxes−κ_i∇T; the common gradient factors out. In series, each layer drops q″ℓ_i/κ_i, so the total drop is q″Σℓ_i/κ_i. Defining q″=κ_effΔT/ℓ yields the harmonic formula and the contact version after adding the jump. The arithmetic value exceeds the harmonic one by the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality applied to positive weighted conductivities and reciprocals. Conservation follows by equating the rate of stored energy in any control volume with incoming boundary flux plus sources, then applying the divergence theorem. These arguments require no Hodge decomposition or unvalidated learned predictor.',
 'Stipulateκ₁=320,f=.12 as retrieved inputs, and chooseκ₂=1 only as a new illustrative contrast.',
 'The parallel value is281.72, while the series value is4000/491≈8.14664 in the same conductivity units. Ifκ₂ were idealized as zero, the parallel limit would be281.6, matching one source number only under that extra idealization; the series limit would instead be zero. The verifier checks the rational formulas and the contact-resistance monotonicity.',
 'The same fractions support radically different exact values depending on transport geometry, so a reported κ_eff needs a declared model and data.',
 'The illustrativeκ₂ is not a material measurement. Arbitrary microstructures need a homogenization theorem or experiment; anisotropy requires a positive-definite conductivity tensor. Numerical metrics such as96.8% prediction accuracy or±2.8% variation are not validated here.',
 'Pose a periodic conductivity cell problem, solve it with certified energy bounds and compare the resulting tensor against series/parallel limits. Interface resistances and uncertainty intervals should enter the same variational formulation.',
 [('MIT intermediate heat and mass transfer','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-51-intermediate-heat-and-mass-transfer-fall-2011/'),('MIT multivariable calculus divergence theorem','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-02sc-multivariable-calculus-fall-2010/')])
add(24,'A bonded-layer stress calculation needs a compatibility model','2025-05-31T03:21:40Z','A','root_late_month.json','A@2025-05-31T03:21:40Z',
 'Under what compatibility and force-balance assumptions can interface thermal stress be calculated, and what remains unverifiable in the stated numerical claims?',
 'A stress value is not determined by thermal expansion coefficients alone. A simple bonded-bar model states its mechanical assumptions and produces an exact stress formula by energy minimization. It is an illustrative calculation rather than validation of the reported274.5MPa or an operational limit.',
 ['Two perfectly bonded axial layers share strainε and temperature changeΔT. Their areas are A_i>0, moduli E_i>0 and expansion coefficientsα_i. Assume no net external axial force, no bending and linear elasticity.',
 'Their stresses areσ_i=E_i(ε−α_iΔT), and stored elastic energy per common length is U(ε)=ΣA_iE_i(ε−α_iΔT)²/2.'],
 'Compatible strain as a unique convex minimizer',
 'The common strain isε*=ΔT(ΣA_iE_iα_i)/(ΣA_iE_i), withΣA_iσ_i=0. For equal A and E, stresses are±E(α₂−α₁)ΔT/2. Changing boundary constraints changes the result.',
 'Differentiate U to obtain U′=ΣA_iE_i(ε−α_iΔT). Setting this to zero is exactly axial force balance. Since U″=ΣA_iE_i>0, the stationary point is the unique global minimizer. Substitution yields the strain formula and equal-layer stresses. If both layers were externally constrained toε=0, the stresses would instead be−E_iα_iΔT, showing why omitted compatibility data cannot be repaired by a more precise multiplication. The stress dimensions are modulus times dimensionless strain; expansion coefficients carry inverse-temperature units.',
 'Choose a declared model with equal layers, E=200GPa,α₂−α₁=10^(−5)K^(−1) andΔT=100K.',
 'The stress magnitudes are100MPa, opposite in sign. The result is exact for these stipulated decimals. Separately the source’s specific-mass figures7.48 and6.8kg/kW differ by exactly10% relative to6.8; without the mass and power denominator neither is established. A reported electrophoretic velocity likewise cannot be recomputed without its field and mobility model.',
 'The example supplies a practical compatibility certificate and identifies which historical numbers lack sufficient inputs.',
 'Actual multilayers can bend, relax, creep, delaminate or have nonuniform temperature. The bar model is not an interface-failure criterion or a substitute for measurements. No strength, flight or deposition recommendation follows.',
 'For a specified laminate, minimize a quadratic energy over midplane strain and curvature and propagate interval material parameters to stress bounds. Compare those bounds with independently measured strength distributions rather than treating one nominal stress as a reliability probability.',
 [('MIT mechanics of materials and thermal stress','https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-001-mechanics-materials-i-fall-2006/')])

